#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162858} :activity/id #uuid "33210971-70cd-50fd-974b-053eab8e1cab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048143 :topic/id #uuid "82e1668e-936b-52f4-a230-dc0cb3dc1f13" :topic/math-academy-id 3133} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163233 :tutorial/content "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/f6/f65e0eee905da85ce0be4bbf39546c2b31c2bac082ff00a6a1d54fd5b89a4a4e.svg)\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.**  Find the singular values of $A.$ Computing the eigenvalues of $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 4, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} =  2,  \\qquad \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} =  \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$). Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_1 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_2 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/07/07085f021218ff17060b3c1a9099e1cabad24b3d12269f97f1fe50d49b970f6b.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$ Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the first two columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $U$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix. However, we do not have the third singular value, so we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n![](images/71/71f89dbcee33bce284b3085b872fdb9da2cc41166fe8a0bd26ef0bb5fe6876aa.svg) \nsuch that \n![](images/9e/9edc744372650e86c27f31ef15ac575cc8203803bdc5fe5730390727fd37146a.svg)\nSolving this system, we get the following nonzero normalized solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c2/c2bbd771d886da6b3a369b7e03345d1be75c18c26c66590598581d2d99590524.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "670f2f0c-5dc5-54ae-9a39-2166ee2414fd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4893 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0ede58be-b8d2-5bb7-a17c-e74da723f67c" :step/math-academy-id 648291 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162041 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161835 :question/id #uuid "16e5cc04-aa74-55f7-992b-b9992a8dd1c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8632"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44c68619-9cf3-5dd8-b3db-00c9730d3308" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050726}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511512}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Third Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "93a722c1-3bdf-59db-908f-c5d0b209511b" :step/math-academy-id 648292 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162042 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161836 :question/id #uuid "bba077a6-56c6-5137-b949-26fae1c98625" :question/math-academy-id "e-8633"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6732620-fad1-5dd3-98c5-07ba59d0b286" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050770}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545815}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "2a223abd-811a-561b-aba4-561597ec202e" :step/math-academy-id 648298 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161837 :question/id #uuid "4fd6d412-d080-5fd1-9318-65a0f5f02dc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8634"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049951}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497345}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "1b7070be-3dac-5359-9175-8300793c3568" :step/math-academy-id 648295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163258 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find an SVD for the $2\\times 4$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/32/325fa7080fdd5f7fc5723a2304aac049a938dfd1b490f09a7a2ceabb0b934db8.svg)\n\nDirectly applying the standard approach to $A$ could be problematic because it requires finding the eigenvalues of the $4\\times 4$ matrix $A^T \\! A.$ \n\nIn such cases, it's usually more convenient to find an SVD of the transposed matrix $A^T$ and then take the transpose of the resulting SVD. In this case, we would only have to find the eigenvalues of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA^T = U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis an SVD for $A^T,$ then\n\n$$\nA= \\left(A^T\\right)^T= \\left(U \\Sigma V^T\\right)^T=V\\Sigma^T U^T\n$$\n\nis an SVD for $A.$  \n\nGoing through our usual process, we find that an SVD for $A^T$ is\n\n![](images/78/7802bcf50374a71efc4c0a7754c5628e88e7f1dfac345bc9e590cfcebd89d8d1.svg)\n\nTaking the transpose of the above equation. we find that an SVD for $A$ is\n\n![](images/77/77f464a1ce372dcd4e9aecb99b23342dee26fd5cf3f701ffa2fc858ebb4865b5.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "71ba93f6-01a1-54c4-bda4-b51b9b8dad77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5110 :tutorial/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a Transposed Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e87c19a4-7e24-5962-880c-39d60fa71758" :step/math-academy-id 648304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161839 :question/id #uuid "1b9d8b05-cdb6-555b-b68c-b46000bb6650" :question/math-academy-id "e-8798"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549020}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"} :step/id #uuid "7cf890cb-057f-5300-9a99-426e3fb959db" :step/math-academy-id 648301 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385971 :tutorial/content "In some singular value decomposition problems, we do not get all of the needed vectors from the usual formulas right away.\n\nThis happens, for example, when:\n\n- an eigenvalue is repeated, or\n\n- a singular value is zero, so the corresponding vector is not determined uniquely.\n\nIn these cases, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Solve the system that defines the eigenspace (or orthogonal complement).  \n\nFor example, we solve an equation of the form\n\n$$\n(A^T A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nor, when finding missing columns of $U,$ we solve orthogonality conditions such as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{u}_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Write the general solution using free variables.  \n\nThis shows that there are infinitely many solutions. For example, the solution might look like\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = x\\,\\mathbf{b}_1 + y\\,\\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are free variables.\n\n- **Step 3.** Choose convenient vectors from the solution space.  \n\nWe usually choose simple values for the free variables to get easy vectors. These vectors should be linearly independent and, if possible, already orthogonal.\n\n- **Step 4.** Orthogonalize the chosen vectors if necessary.  \n\nIf the vectors are not mutually orthogonal, we apply the Gram–Schmidt process.\n\n- **Step 5.** Normalize the vectors.  \n\nAfter that, we divide each vector by its norm to obtain unit vectors.\n\nThese unit vectors form an orthonormal basis for the solution space, and they can be used as the needed columns of $V$ or $U.$\n\n**Note:** In these situations, there is usually *more than one correct answer*. Different choices of free variables can produce different orthonormal bases, so the resulting SVD is not necessarily unique. What matters is that the chosen vectors:\n\n- belong to the correct solution space,\n\n- are mutually orthogonal, and\n\n- have norm $1.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "5e93068a-566a-5c34-a459-3250d8a847d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15621 :tutorial/title "Choosing Eigenvectors When Eigenvalues Repeat or Are Missing"} :step/id #uuid "7dc02cbe-4a5d-5f2d-8ad3-baa5e8278916" :step/math-academy-id 648305 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162946}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of Larger Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162227}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408023} :activity/id #uuid "069cd187-e43e-5701-8359-21c488d6fdf7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048143 :topic/id #uuid "82e1668e-936b-52f4-a230-dc0cb3dc1f13" :topic/math-academy-id 3133} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1624144f-dc91-5236-84ee-18b96e8277af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407934} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0a89b3-42e1-5c0c-9f1d-976b0b80e3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407939} {:answer/id #uuid "746511ed-c96a-5c0b-a954-73fab4f89071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407934} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef2a40d-dd8f-5054-9101-9b4095ee0f14" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e47d3ea-4750-55d7-9ad9-f144dbee61aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407943} {:answer/id #uuid "9c719bc8-cc87-56ef-930a-6bf23b501c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407975} {:answer/id #uuid "cea590ba-fbf3-55d0-a265-d70d43b6166d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407988} {:answer/id #uuid "de95b675-12ca-5166-875b-1b757ac71752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb